WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.237
Module Three, Lesson Two: LLM as a judge.

2
00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:05.970
It's powerful, as long as you know how it fails.

3
00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:10.432
Picture a news app whose agent writes ten thousand summaries a day.

4
00:00:10.762 --> 00:00:14.787
Good summaries never match word for word, and no editor can read them all.

5
00:00:15.587 --> 00:00:18.310
So the team has another model grade every summary.

6
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.491
That's LLM as a judge: a scorer that scales.

7
00:00:22.291 --> 00:00:25.591
Judges shine where quality is real but can't be string-matched.

8
00:00:25.921 --> 00:00:29.988
Think summary quality, tone, helpfulness, or groundedness in a source.

9
00:00:30.788 --> 00:00:33.895
But if a deterministic check exists, use code instead.

10
00:00:34.225 --> 00:00:36.146
Schemas, numbers, passing tests,

11
00:00:36.196 --> 00:00:40.149
or the right tool call are cheaper, faster, and unbiased in code.

12
00:00:40.949 --> 00:00:43.459
Next, learn to name the judge's four biases.

13
00:00:43.789 --> 00:00:48.155
Position bias favors the first answer, so score both orders and average.

14
00:00:48.955 --> 00:00:52.233
In the news app, the top-rated summaries were simply the longest.

15
00:00:52.563 --> 00:00:56.886
That's verbosity bias, fixed with a rubric that rewards information density.

16
00:00:57.686 --> 00:01:01.668
The same model family also wrote and judged, inflating every score.

17
00:01:01.998 --> 00:01:05.212
That's self-preference, so use a judge from a different family.

18
00:01:06.012 --> 00:01:09.397
Score compression clusters everything at seven or eight out of ten.

19
00:01:09.727 --> 00:01:13.858
Pairwise comparison or a short discrete rubric spreads the scores out.

20
00:01:14.658 --> 00:01:16.699
Now, design a judge that holds up.

21
00:01:17.029 --> 00:01:18.989
Ask which of two answers is better,

22
00:01:19.039 --> 00:01:22.781
and anchor the rubric with examples of a one, a three, and a five.

23
00:01:23.581 --> 00:01:26.241
Make the judge write its reasoning before its score.

24
00:01:26.571 --> 00:01:30.019
Scoring first turns the reasoning into an after-the-fact excuse.

25
00:01:30.819 --> 00:01:34.124
Then calibrate on one to two hundred expert-labeled cases,

26
00:01:34.174 --> 00:01:36.380
measuring agreement with Cohen's kappa.

27
00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.905
A judge agreeing only sixty percent of the time is measuring something else.

28
00:01:41.705 --> 00:01:45.026
Finally, pin the judge's version like any other dependency.

29
00:01:45.356 --> 00:01:48.954
A silent provider upgrade would change your whole metric history.

30
00:01:49.754 --> 00:01:52.491
NVIDIA's NeMo Evaluator runs benchmarks,

31
00:01:52.541 --> 00:01:56.253
custom datasets, and judges as comparable scored runs.

32
00:01:56.583 --> 00:02:00.437
After a fine-tune, it answers: did the new model beat the incumbent?

33
00:02:01.237 --> 00:02:05.689
Exam tip: if one option is a code check instead of a judge, pick the code.

34
00:02:06.019 --> 00:02:10.897
And suspiciously high scores point to self-preference; read on for all four biases.
